+*   **When to use:** Real-time global illumination, voxel cone tracing, volumetric effects where speed is prioritized over perfect geometric accuracy [S5, unofficial].
 *   **When not to use:** When sparse storage is strictly required (use octree variants), or when analytical precision is needed without rasterization artifacts.
 
 ## Background
-This method originated as a practical implementation of the first step of Voxel Cone Tracing, a technique for real-time global illumination [S3, S4, unofficial]. It was popularized by blog posts and source code from developers like Alexandre Pestana, Simon Yeung, and Alex Nankervis, who adapted hardware rasterization for 3D grids [S4, unofficial]. The core insight is that the GPU rasterizer can be "tricked" into filling a 3D volume by projecting triangles along their dominant axis, effectively treating the 3D grid as a series of 2D layers [S4, unofficial]. It is widely used in modern real-time rendering pipelines for GI and volumetric effects [S4, unofficial].
